{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","id":"msg-2","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"**The Last Light at Gannet Point**\n\nFor thirty-one years, Eamon Vell climbed the one hundred and twelve iron steps of Gannet Point every evening before sunset, and every evening he lit the lamp.\n\nThe light had been automated in 1987. The mainland office said the keeper's post was no longer needed, that a sensor and a timer could do the work. Eamon had agreed, politely, and then kept coming back anyway. He told himself it was to check the glass and oil the gears. The truth was that the tower had become the one place where his wife's absence felt like a shape he could walk around.\n\nMarian had kept the cottage below the cliffs with him for twenty-six years. She had hung laundry on the wire, baked bread that tasted faintly of sea salt, and sung off-key while she sorted the mail boat's post. She died in February, quietly, in the hospital in town. Eamon had not been able to stand the silence of the cottage since then, so he spent his nights in the tower, watching the water.\n\nOn the first evening of November, a storm came in from the west, low and fast. Eamon was already at the top when the wind began to shriek against the glass. He checked the timer. It had failed, as it sometimes did, its small green display blinking an error. He swore, then laughed at himself for swearing at a machine, and turned the manual crank on the lamp mechanism by hand, the way he had been taught as a young man.\n\nThe beam swept out over the black water. Somewhere to the south, a fishing boat was running for shelter. He could not see it, but the radio crackled with a voice he didn't recognize, a young man asking whether anyone could see the light.\n\n\"I see you,\" Eamon said into the handset. \"Keep it to the left of the beam. Steady as you go.\"\n\nHe talked the boat in for nearly two hours, calling bearings, describing the rocks, reading the swell by the way the spray broke against the headland. The young skipper's voice grew steadier. Eamon's own hands cramped on the crank, but he did not stop. Each rotation felt like an answer to a question he had been asking all year.\n\nNear midnight, the boat slid past the point and into the lee of the harbor wall. The skipper's voice came back over the radio, hoarse and grateful. \"Thanks, Keeper. Thanks. We wouldn't have made it.\"\n\nEamon sat down on the cold iron floor and rested his back against the lamp housing. The storm was still roaring outside, but inside the tower it had become quiet.\n\nIn the morning the wind dropped and the sea went flat and silver. The mainland office sent a technician out on the afternoon boat to fix the timer. He found the old keeper sitting at the top of the tower with a thermos of tea, looking out at the water.\n\n\"You didn't have to do it by hand,\" the technician said. \"The system would have run fine if we'd gotten here sooner.\"\n\nEamon smiled, but not in a way that asked for argument. \"I know. But someone had to hold the light.\"\n\nThe technician packed his tools and left. Eamon stayed a while longer. Then he rose, stiff-kneed, and began the long walk down the one hundred and twelve steps. At the bottom, he stopped by the cottage window and saw the laundry line, still strung between the posts, empty in the thin winter sun.\n\nHe did not take the line down. He went inside, put the kettle on, and for the first time in months, he hummed a little, badly, the way Marian used to."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1906,"cache_read_input_tokens":3394,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1906,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu-1","agent_id":